  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We propose a multi-resolution optimization strategy that combines randomized search and deterministic optimization to tackle the non-convex Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The central idea is to leverage different discretization levels to escape local optima while maintaining reasonable computational complexity.\n\n   Currently, the best known bound is 0.38085 achieved with 70-point samples. The bottleneck in previous approaches is the optimization at high resolutions, which suffers from O(n\u00b2) convolution operations per candidate evaluation. We address this by:\n   - Using a two-phase optimization: coarse then fine-grained resolution searches\n   - Implementing an efficient projection mechanism that preserves the integral constraint\n   - Adapting the optimization budget dynamically based on resolution scaling\n   \n   The approach differs from previous strategies by focusing on resolution adaptation and randomized search at lower resolutions before committing to higher resolutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\n   Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   Subject to: \n     0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x \u2208 [0,2]\n     \u222b_0^2 h(x)dx = 1\n\n   Discretization:\n     n_points = 2^k samples (typically 70, 140, 280, etc.)\n     dx = 2.0 / n_points\n     Integral constraint: \u03a3h_i * dx = 1  \u21e8 \u03a3h_i = n_points/2\n\n   Key helper functions:\n     def project(h: ndarray, n_points: int) -> ndarray: \n        \"\"\"Projects h to satisfy [0,1] range and integral constraint\"\"\"\n        s = np.sum(h)\n        factor = (n_points/2) / s\n        h_scaled = h * factor\n        h_clipped = np.clip(h_scaled, 0, 1)\n        s_clipped = np.sum(h_clipped)\n        if abs(s_clipped) < 1e-5:  # Prevent division by zero\n            h_final = np.ones_like(h) * (n_points/2 / n_points)\n        else:\n            h_final = h_clipped * (n_points/2 / s_clipped)\n        return np.clip(h_final, 0, 1)\n\n     def compute_C5(h: ndarray, n_points: int) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Compute overlap integral bound with projection\"\"\"\n        h_proj = project(h, n_points)\n        conv = np.correlate(h_proj, 1-h_proj, mode=\"full\")\n        return (np.max(conv) * (2.0 / n_points))\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n   For each resolution n_points (starting from 70):\n   - Generate random samples in [0,1] or reuse parent solution\n   - Run RandomizedSearch:\n        n_iter = 1000 + (n_points-70)//10  # Budget adaptation\n        candidates = []  # Store (score, h) from random search\n        for _ in range(n_iter):\n            h_rand = np.random.uniform(0,1,n_points)\n            score = compute_C5(h_rand, n_points)\n            best_candidate = (score, h_rand) if score < best_score else best_candidate\n            candidates.append(score)\n            \n        # Track personal best & global best\n\n   - Use BestIndividual optimizer with linear mutation:\n        class BestIndividual:\n            def __init__(self, dimensions, seed=42):\n                self.pos = np.random.rand(dimensions)\n                self.score = float('inf')\n                self.dim = dimensions\n                random = np.random.RandomState(seed)\n                self.mutation = random.normal(0, 0.3, dimensions)\n                \n            def update(self, candidates):\n                # Update mutation vector with better samples\n                idx = random.choice(candidates) - self.pos\n                self.mutation += idx * 0.2\n                pass\n            \n            def evolve(self, archive):\n                # Linear mutation\n                new_pos = self.pos + self.mutation * 0.2\n                new_pos = np.clip(new_pos, 0, 1)\n                return new_pos\n\n   - After 100 iterations, switch to deterministic optimization:\n        Use CasADi-based interior point solver for promising samples\n\n   - Dynamic budget allocation:\n        Base time estimate: 0.5 + (n_points**1.5)/1000 seconds per candidate\n        Stop if remaining budget $<\\text{estimate}$ for next resolution\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n   - Resolution doubling schedule: Start with 70, then 140, 280,...\n   - Population size: 20 best individuals per resolution level\n   - Mutation rate scaling: Adaptive mutation based on n_points\n   - Algorithmic variations:\n        1. init=\"parents\" variation: Use parent interpolation for higher resolutions\n        2. warmup=\"1000\" variation: Run 1000 iterations before committing to optimization\n        3. fine_tune=seed variation: Perform local optimization after global search\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n   - Memory usage: O(n_points^2) for convolution at resolution n\n   - Time usage: \n        Time(resolution) = (resolution^1.5)/500 \n        Each resolution level should take <= 20 seconds base estimate\n    - Failure modes:\n        NaN scores handled by fallback to average initialization\n        Out-of-memory errors trigger resolution fallback\n        Poor local optima detected by score crevasse monitoring\n\n   - Fallback strategy:\n        best_candidate = array of length 70 (parent solution)\n        score = 0.38085\n        remains valid\n\n   - Timeout handling:\n        Implement per-candidate maxtime in sampler\n        Stop and return best_candidate if:\n            budget_s - t0 < time_estimate*2\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n   For all candidates:\n      validate_erdos(h, n_points):\n          assert min(h)>=-1e-7\n          assert max(h)<=1+1e-7\n          x = np.sum(h)\n          assert abs(x - n_points/2) < 3  # normalized\n\n   Return contract for run():\n      (best_h, best_score, n_points)  # unzipped\n      best_score must be <= parent's 0.38085\n      best_h must be valid via validate_erdos\n\n   San\u25a1ity checks included:\n      Check integral constraint\n      Check positive definite overlap metric\n      Check convolution length (2*n_points-1)",
